### the adversarial construction: k1 = lcm(15..Q), k2 = 2*k1 ###
  Q=20   lcm(15..Q) has 6 digits ; legal: False (not covering)
        killing at 6 of 6 moduli in [15,20]  -> criterion has a witness: False
  Q=25   lcm(15..Q) has 10 digits ; legal: False (not covering)
        killing at 11 of 11 moduli in [15,25]  -> criterion has a witness: False
  Q=30   lcm(15..Q) has 13 digits ; legal: True  (legal trapped-core shape)
        killing at 16 of 16 moduli in [15,30]  -> criterion has a witness: False
  Q=40   lcm(15..Q) has 16 digits ; legal: True  (legal trapped-core shape)
        killing at 26 of 26 moduli in [15,40]  -> criterion has a witness: False
  Q=60   lcm(15..Q) has 25 digits ; legal: True  (legal trapped-core shape)
        killing at 46 of 46 moduli in [15,60]  -> criterion has a witness: False

### is the criterion's FAILURE fatal?  scale separation of these families ###
  Q=20  killers L, 2L with L ~ 10^5 ; core max = 12
        killer/core scale ratio = L/12 ~ 10^4 ; killer-killer ratio = 2 (comparable)
  Q=30  killers L, 2L with L ~ 10^12 ; core max = 12
        killer/core scale ratio = L/12 ~ 10^11 ; killer-killer ratio = 2 (comparable)

### how SMALL can a killer pair be and still kill every modulus in [15,Q]? ###
  Q    min max(k1,k2) killing all of [15,Q]      example
  18   271                                    (182,271)
  20   1274                                   (161,1274)
  22   1274                                   (881,1274)
  24   > 4000 (none found)                    -
  26   > 4000 (none found)                    -
  28   > 4000 (none found)                    -
DONE
